/** @page odbcreleasenotesguide Release Notes

    @tableofcontents

    @anchor odbcv1_2_0
    @section odbcv2_0_0 odbc Driver Version 2.0.0

    @subsection odbcv2_overview Overview

    Version 2.0 adds native typed and columnar query results and improves connection diagnostics.
    See @ref odbccapabilitiesguide for driver capabilities and @ref odbcschemaguide for result metadata.

    @subsection odbcv2_features New Features

    - Return the underlying ODBC driver name with @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::getDriverRealName() "Datasource::getDriverRealName()"
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4690">issue 4690</a>)
    - Native typed results through @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectTyped() "Datasource::selectTyped()" and
      @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectRowsTyped() "Datasource::selectRowsTyped()" when built with the corresponding Qore APIs
    - Columnar results through @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectColumnar() "Datasource::selectColumnar()" and
      @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement::fetchColumnar() "SQLStatement::fetchColumnar()" when built with columnar Qore APIs,
      including dense buffers for integer, floating-point, and safe numeric/decimal columns

    @subsection odbcv2_fixes Bug Fixes
    - did not add the \c "preserve-case" option to the ODBC connection string, as it is an option of this driver
      and not of the ODBC driver; with \c NULL as its value, the connection could not be opened
    - bound a \c NULL or \c NOTHING argument, or a missing argument, for a \c %v placeholder as \c NULL; the
      driver could get fewer parameters than the command has placeholders and fail with an error such as
      <tt>[07002] The # of IPD parameters 1 < 2 the # of parameter markers</tt>
    - validated timeout syntax and range, accepted zero explicitly, and checked connection attribute errors
    - requested native connection cancellation and preserved pending Qore cancellation after native connection returns


    - Read every result of SQL that returns more than one result, such as a batch of statements or a stored
      procedure; previously only the first result was read, so later result sets and row counts were lost and,
      with SQL Server, errors of later statements were silently ignored.  An error in any statement is now raised,
      @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::exec() "Datasource::exec()" and @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::select() "Datasource::select()"
      return every result set and row count, and calls that return one result set raise
      \c ODBC-MULTIPLE-RESULT-SETS-ERROR for a second result set instead of discarding it
      (see @ref odbc_multiple_results)
    - SQL without a result set passed to @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectRows() "Datasource::selectRows()",
      @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectRowsTyped() "Datasource::selectRowsTyped()" or
      @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::selectRow() "Datasource::selectRow()" raises \c ODBC-SELECT-ROWS-ERROR or
      \c ODBC-SELECT-ROW-ERROR ("SQL passed to selectRows() did not return a result set") where the ODBC driver
      refuses to fetch from a row count; it raised \c ODBC-FETCH-ERROR with the driver's "invalid cursor state"
      error; where the driver returns no rows, no rows are returned as before
    - A @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement "SQLStatement" no longer leaves a SQL Server connection busy after its result
      set has been read; @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement::affectedRows() "SQLStatement::affectedRows()" returns the
      total row count of a batch
    - Return the total row count of DML executed with parameter arrays; with drivers that report a row count for
      each parameter set, such as psqlODBC, only the first set's count was returned
    - A program interrupt cancels an execution, a fetch, or a wait for a later result of a batch that is blocked
      on the server (see @ref odbc_cancellation)
    - A @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement "SQLStatement" can be executed again before the results of its previous
      execution have been read; this failed with an "invalid cursor state" or "cursor is open" error
    - Keep bound parameter values in place until the statement is executed; when more than one value of a type
      (or more than 32 strings) was bound in one statement, the driver read earlier values from released memory,
      so wrong values could be stored, or the statement could fail or leave the connection unusable
    - Show SQL text, object names and server messages unchanged in exception descriptions; a \c % character in
      them was interpreted as a format directive, which garbled the message or crashed the process
    - Bind strings and binary values longer than the driver's maximum for character and binary parameters as
      long data (see @ref odbc_long_values); with SQL Server, values over 8000 bytes were refused with
      \c HY104 by the Microsoft driver and silently truncated to 8000 bytes by FreeTDS
    - Closing a connection no longer hangs when the driver refuses to disconnect: a statement still waiting for
      data is cancelled and freed, an active transaction is rolled back, and the connection is disconnected once;
      the driver was called again every 50 milliseconds without end.  When built with %Qore 3.0 or later, a
      connection that the driver still cannot disconnect raises \c ODBC-DISCONNECT-ERROR where it is closed, for
      example by @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::close() "Datasource::close()" or the destruction of the datasource
      (@ref Qore::odbc::ODBC_HAVE_DISCONNECT_ERROR "ODBC_HAVE_DISCONNECT_ERROR" is \c True then); it was not reported
      at all
    - A commit or rollback on a connection that the driver reports as lost (for example after the server ended the
      session) raises \c ODBC-CONNECTION-DEAD-ERROR; the PostgreSQL ODBC driver reports success for them, so the
      commit of a transaction that was lost with the connection appeared to succeed
    - An execution that the driver answers with \c SQL_NEED_DATA (the driver asking for parameter data at execution,
      which this driver never binds) is cancelled and raises \c ODBC-EXEC-ERROR saying so; the statement was left
      half-executed, which made it unusable and kept the connection from being used or closed
    - Build in C++17 mode, as the %Qore headers require; building from source needs a C++17 compiler
    - Preserve SQL NULL values in all-NULL, floating-point, and boolean parameter arrays
    - Allocate valid storage for empty-string and empty-binary arrays and scalar broadcasts
    - Check array buffer size arithmetic and cooperative cancellation while preparing string and binary arrays
    - Propagate scalar NULL array binding errors immediately; reject mixed absolute/relative date arrays safely
    - Mask connection passwords in \c ODBC-CONNECTION-ERROR exception messages
    - Correct swapped \c login-timeout and \c connection-timeout values returned by
      @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::getOption() "Datasource::getOption()"
    - Correct the error message for an invalid \c connection-timeout option and stop processing after validation
      failures in @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::setOption() "Datasource::setOption()"
    - Handle missing driver-name information safely in @ref Qore::SQL::Datasource::getDriverRealName() "Datasource::getDriverRealName()"

    @section odbcv1_1_3 odbc Driver Version 1.1.3
    - fixed handling retrieving 0-length \c VARCHAR values
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4681">issue 4681</a>)

    @section odbcv1_1_2 odbc Driver Version 1.1.2
    - update to fix builds with the updated \c qpp from %Qore

    @section odbcv1_1_1 odbc Driver Version 1.1.1
    - fixed a bug iterating result sets with the @ref Qore::SQL::SQLStatement "SQLStatement" class
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4616">issue 4616</a>)
    - fixed handling character encoding issues; strings are bound and retrieved using the DB encoding for the
      datasource; eliminated special logic for handling UTF-16 strings that caused crashes in some cases
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4615">issue 4615</a>)
    - fixed a bug where the \c conn option would be used to build the connection, but the value was not returned as an
      option value
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4613">issue 4613</a>)

    @section odbcv1_1 odbc Driver Version 1.1
    - automatically strip trailing spaces from \c CHAR column values
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4508">issue 4508</a>)
    - updated to run with the latest unixODBC versions
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4507">issue 4507</a>)

    @section odbcv1_0 odbc Driver Version 1.0
    - initial release
*/
